1
00:00:00,333 --> 00:00:01,853
What Is One CEO Worth?

2
00:00:01,853 --> 00:00:06,646
Nvidia Just Found Out November 12, 2026.

3
00:00:06,806 --> 00:00:10,886
The man who started Nvidia in a diner
booth thirty-three years ago resigns

4
00:00:10,886 --> 00:00:14,006
as its chief executive,
effective immediately.

5
00:00:14,621 --> 00:00:18,581
In the days around that announcement,
Nvidia's stock falls about seventeen

6
00:00:18,581 --> 00:00:19,581
percent.

7
00:00:19,501 --> 00:00:24,301
According to CNN, about seven hundred
fifty billion dollars of market value

8
00:00:24,301 --> 00:00:25,301
disappears.

9
00:00:25,433 --> 00:00:29,753
That's more than the entire value of
almost every company on Earth.

10
00:00:29,993 --> 00:00:31,913
But the chips didn't change.

11
00:00:31,913 --> 00:00:33,674
The factories didn't change.

12
00:00:33,674 --> 00:00:37,754
Five days later, the company
reported its biggest quarter ever.

13
00:00:37,754 --> 00:00:40,314
So what exactly did investors lose?

14
00:00:40,314 --> 00:00:43,357
How much of a company's value
can one person carry?

15
00:00:43,437 --> 00:00:45,837
We're going to follow the
money and find out.

16
00:00:45,837 --> 00:00:47,597
This is Billions and Bulls.

17
00:00:47,597 --> 00:00:49,517
Let's follow the money.

18
00:00:50,557 --> 00:00:53,403
Part 1 The Booth That Launched a Giant

19
00:00:53,643 --> 00:00:58,683
In 1993, three engineers met at a Denny's
restaurant in San Jose, California,

20
00:00:58,683 --> 00:01:02,043
to plan a company that would make
graphics chips for video games.

21
00:01:02,043 --> 00:01:03,734
One of them was Jensen Huang.

22
00:01:03,894 --> 00:01:08,774
He became the chief executive, and stayed
in the job for thirty-three years.

23
00:01:09,094 --> 00:01:13,414
For most of that time, Nvidia was a company
gamers knew and investors mostly

24
00:01:13,414 --> 00:01:14,414
ignored.

25
00:01:14,329 --> 00:01:17,929
Then artificial intelligence turned out
to run best on the same kind of

26
00:01:17,929 --> 00:01:18,929
chips.

27
00:01:18,729 --> 00:01:22,075
Suddenly, every big tech company
wanted Nvidia's hardware.

28
00:01:22,235 --> 00:01:26,395
And for a while, almost nobody else
could make it at the same scale.

29
00:01:26,715 --> 00:01:30,566
In early October 2026, Nvidia's
shares hit a record.

30
00:01:30,726 --> 00:01:34,886
The company was worth about five
point eight trillion dollars.

31
00:01:35,286 --> 00:01:37,606
Huang became the face of all of it.

32
00:01:37,606 --> 00:01:41,206
The leather jacket, the keynote speeches,
the deals with governments and

33
00:01:41,206 --> 00:01:42,206
tech giants.

34
00:01:42,318 --> 00:01:47,172
According to Nvidia's own filing from March
2026, he owned about three point

35
00:01:47,172 --> 00:01:48,798
six percent of the company.

36
00:01:48,798 --> 00:01:54,222
At the October peak, that stake was worth
roughly two hundred billion dollars.

37
00:01:54,222 --> 00:01:56,622
But here's a detail that matters later.

38
00:01:56,622 --> 00:02:00,542
Unlike some famous founders, Huang didn't
control the company through special

39
00:02:00,542 --> 00:02:01,582
voting shares.

40
00:02:01,582 --> 00:02:03,890
Nvidia has one class of stock.

41
00:02:04,050 --> 00:02:07,970
His power came from his track record, and
from a board that trusted him.

42
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:11,490
Which means that board
could also let him go.

43
00:02:11,490 --> 00:02:15,503
In 2023, Denny's put a plaque on
that booth in San Jose.

44
00:02:15,503 --> 00:02:19,103
It celebrated the place where a
trillion-dollar company was born.

45
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:23,823
On November 12, 2026, according to
reports, the plaque came off the wall.

46
00:02:23,823 --> 00:02:26,516
A day later, it went back up.

47
00:02:27,396 --> 00:02:30,276
Part 2 Two Weeks in November

48
00:02:30,276 --> 00:02:32,916
Here's what happened, based on
the reporting so far.

49
00:02:32,916 --> 00:02:37,327
And a warning first: a lot of this is
still being investigated, and some

50
00:02:37,327 --> 00:02:39,087
of it is disputed.

51
00:02:39,687 --> 00:02:43,688
On October 29, The New York Times
reported that Huang had been the target

52
00:02:43,688 --> 00:02:46,228
of a possible spy plot linked to China.

53
00:02:46,228 --> 00:02:50,308
According to the Times, he'd had an
extramarital relationship with a former

54
00:02:50,308 --> 00:02:53,028
executive of a Singapore
data center company.

55
00:02:53,028 --> 00:02:55,084
A company that bought Nvidia chips.

56
00:02:55,244 --> 00:02:59,404
US officials, the Times reported, suspected
she had been recruited by Chinese

57
00:02:59,404 --> 00:03:00,404
intelligence.

58
00:03:00,284 --> 00:03:03,084
China called the claims
groundless speculation.

59
00:03:03,084 --> 00:03:06,591
The company said the reports weren't
supported by verifiable evidence.

60
00:03:06,751 --> 00:03:10,351
And the US Justice Department later said
it had reached no conclusion about

61
00:03:10,351 --> 00:03:12,272
whether she was a spy.

62
00:03:12,591 --> 00:03:16,863
Huang said Nvidia's dealings with that
company were completely normal, and

63
00:03:16,863 --> 00:03:20,171
that Nvidia had checked that its
chips were not going to China.

64
00:03:20,331 --> 00:03:23,771
In Singapore, where the company is
based, the authorities said they did

65
00:03:23,771 --> 00:03:27,051
not have enough evidence of the offences
they had looked into there, and

66
00:03:27,051 --> 00:03:30,549
that they had not
investigated anyone for spying.

67
00:03:31,110 --> 00:03:35,269
Then, on November 11, the Times reported
that Huang had also had a relationship

68
00:03:35,269 --> 00:03:38,069
with an executive who
reported directly to him.

69
00:03:38,069 --> 00:03:40,322
That same day, Nvidia's board met.

70
00:03:40,322 --> 00:03:43,682
On November 12, the company
announced that Huang had resigned.

71
00:03:43,682 --> 00:03:47,602
As chief executive, as
president, and from the board.

72
00:03:47,922 --> 00:03:49,330
The board moved fast.

73
00:03:49,330 --> 00:03:53,570
Colette Kress, the chief financial officer
for thirteen years, became interim

74
00:03:53,570 --> 00:03:54,770
chief executive.

75
00:03:54,770 --> 00:03:58,601
And for the first time in its history,
Nvidia got a separate board chair.

76
00:03:58,601 --> 00:04:02,440
The board said it would search for a
permanent leader, inside and outside

77
00:04:02,440 --> 00:04:03,440
the company.

78
00:04:03,320 --> 00:04:07,656
Kress isn't a household name, but she
isn't new to big companies either.

79
00:04:07,656 --> 00:04:12,536
Before Nvidia, she spent almost
twenty-five years in finance jobs at Cisco

80
00:04:12,536 --> 00:04:13,896
and Microsoft.

81
00:04:14,536 --> 00:04:15,986
The reaction was split.

82
00:04:16,146 --> 00:04:17,826
More than one hundred seventy thousand

83
00:04:18,466 --> 00:04:22,146
people signed an open letter asking
the board to bring Huang back.

84
00:04:22,146 --> 00:04:24,241
Several tech leaders publicly backed him.

85
00:04:24,241 --> 00:04:28,721
On November 17, Huang said he would be
honored to return, if the board decided

86
00:04:28,721 --> 00:04:29,721
that was best.

87
00:04:29,845 --> 00:04:33,045
The same day, the board said it did
not intend to reinstate him.

88
00:04:33,045 --> 00:04:37,205
And on December 1, he's expected to
testify in front of Congress.

89
00:04:38,645 --> 00:04:41,019
Part 3 The Price of One Person

90
00:04:41,259 --> 00:04:42,859
So let's look at the money.

91
00:04:42,859 --> 00:04:47,019
CNN put the drop since Huang admitted the
affair and the investigation began

92
00:04:47,019 --> 00:04:48,939
at about seventeen percent.

93
00:04:48,939 --> 00:04:52,422
Seven hundred fifty-one billion
dollars of market value.

94
00:04:53,061 --> 00:04:56,902
To understand why a company can lose that
much over one person, you need

95
00:04:56,902 --> 00:04:59,141
to know what a share price really is.

96
00:04:59,141 --> 00:05:02,093
A share isn't paying for what
a company owns today.

97
00:05:02,093 --> 00:05:05,612
It's a bet on everything the
company will earn in the future.

98
00:05:05,612 --> 00:05:10,183
Years and years of future profits,
squeezed into one number.

99
00:05:10,663 --> 00:05:14,583
So when a founder leaves, investors
start asking new questions.

100
00:05:14,583 --> 00:05:16,343
Who makes the big bets now?

101
00:05:16,343 --> 00:05:19,623
Who closes the next deal with a
government, or a tech giant?

102
00:05:19,623 --> 00:05:21,472
Will the best engineers stay?

103
00:05:21,712 --> 00:05:25,792
Every unanswered question makes that
future a little less certain.

104
00:05:25,792 --> 00:05:29,632
And a less certain future is
worth less today.

105
00:05:29,792 --> 00:05:31,152
There's a name for this.

106
00:05:31,152 --> 00:05:32,461
Key person risk.

107
00:05:32,461 --> 00:05:35,581
Companies even warn about it in
their annual reports.

108
00:05:35,581 --> 00:05:39,421
It's the risk that the business depends
so much on one or a few people,

109
00:05:39,421 --> 00:05:41,549
that losing them would hurt.

110
00:05:41,869 --> 00:05:44,989
It has happened before, at a
much smaller scale.

111
00:05:44,989 --> 00:05:49,629
In 2019, McDonald's fired its chief
executive, Steve Easterbrook, for a

112
00:05:49,629 --> 00:05:53,460
consensual relationship with an
employee, which broke company policy.

113
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:57,620
He left with a severance package estimated
at around forty million dollars.

114
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:01,074
Then the company sued, saying he had
lied about other relationships.

115
00:06:01,074 --> 00:06:05,714
In 2021, he agreed to return more than
one hundred five million dollars,

116
00:06:05,714 --> 00:06:07,155
and apologized.

117
00:06:08,035 --> 00:06:11,093
The lesson boards took from
cases like that is simple.

118
00:06:11,253 --> 00:06:14,693
Rules about relationships at work
apply at the very top too.

119
00:06:14,693 --> 00:06:19,254
Maybe especially at the top, where one
person's choices can move billions.

120
00:06:19,894 --> 00:06:22,283
But history also has the opposite lesson.

121
00:06:22,283 --> 00:06:26,763
When Steve Jobs stepped down at Apple in
2011, many people wondered if the

122
00:06:26,763 --> 00:06:29,162
company could survive without its founder.

123
00:06:29,162 --> 00:06:33,428
Under his successor, Tim Cook, Apple went on
to become one of the most valuable

124
00:06:33,428 --> 00:06:34,468
companies in the world.

125
00:06:34,468 --> 00:06:35,908
A founder leaving is a risk.

126
00:06:35,908 --> 00:06:37,668
It isn't a sentence.

127
00:06:38,148 --> 00:06:42,388
If you're enjoying the story so far,
subscribing helps the channel a lot.

128
00:06:42,628 --> 00:06:46,468
Because next, we'll find out who actually
lost those seven hundred fifty

129
00:06:46,468 --> 00:06:48,069
billion dollars.

130
00:06:49,108 --> 00:06:52,457
Part 4 Who Lost the $751 Billion

131
00:06:52,777 --> 00:06:55,657
When a stock falls, the money
doesn't go to anyone.

132
00:06:55,657 --> 00:06:59,097
There's no pile of cash that moves
from one place to another.

133
00:06:59,097 --> 00:07:02,206
The value just isn't there
anymore, at least on paper.

134
00:07:02,206 --> 00:07:04,766
But somebody's account balance goes down.

135
00:07:04,766 --> 00:07:06,206
So whose?

136
00:07:06,846 --> 00:07:08,606
Start with Huang himself.

137
00:07:08,606 --> 00:07:09,661
Some simple math.

138
00:07:09,901 --> 00:07:13,661
Three point six percent of seven hundred
fifty-one billion dollars is about

139
00:07:13,661 --> 00:07:15,021
twenty-seven billion.

140
00:07:15,021 --> 00:07:19,483
On paper, the person who lost the most
from his own exit was probably him.

141
00:07:19,963 --> 00:07:24,203
Then there are Nvidia's employees, many of
whom are paid partly in company

142
00:07:24,203 --> 00:07:25,203
stock.

143
00:07:24,843 --> 00:07:27,643
For years, that stock made a
lot of them rich.

144
00:07:27,643 --> 00:07:29,921
In November, it went the other way.

145
00:07:30,401 --> 00:07:33,441
And then, there's almost everyone else.

146
00:07:33,441 --> 00:07:36,828
Nvidia is one of the biggest
companies in the S&P 500.

147
00:07:36,828 --> 00:07:41,309
In mid-November, according to one
analysis, it made up about six percent

148
00:07:41,309 --> 00:07:43,789
of an S&P 500 index fund.

149
00:07:43,789 --> 00:07:48,015
So if you have a retirement account with an
index fund, there's a good chance

150
00:07:48,015 --> 00:07:50,255
you owned a small piece of that drop.

151
00:07:50,255 --> 00:07:54,015
Without ever buying a single
Nvidia share on purpose.

152
00:07:54,495 --> 00:07:55,935
But here's the twist.

153
00:07:55,935 --> 00:07:58,031
Not all of that fall was about Huang.

154
00:07:58,191 --> 00:08:01,951
Nvidia's shares had already started
sliding before the scandal broke.

155
00:08:01,951 --> 00:08:06,351
Since the record in early October, CNN
reports, the stock was down about

156
00:08:06,351 --> 00:08:07,871
thirty-five percent.

157
00:08:07,871 --> 00:08:09,786
Roughly two trillion dollars.

158
00:08:09,786 --> 00:08:13,706
Part of that was a wider fear about the
artificial intelligence boom.

159
00:08:13,706 --> 00:08:18,416
On October 29, the same day the Times story
came out, Google's parent company

160
00:08:18,416 --> 00:08:22,016
had the worst day in its
history on the stock market.

161
00:08:22,416 --> 00:08:26,897
So the honest answer is that nobody can
split the drop neatly into Huang's

162
00:08:26,897 --> 00:08:28,186
part and the market's part.

163
00:08:28,346 --> 00:08:30,026
Investors were nervous already.

164
00:08:30,026 --> 00:08:32,906
The scandal gave them one more reason.

165
00:08:34,186 --> 00:08:36,746
Part 5 The Company Without Him

166
00:08:36,746 --> 00:08:38,074
Then came the test.

167
00:08:38,074 --> 00:08:43,434
On November 17, five days after the
resignation, Nvidia reported its quarterly

168
00:08:43,434 --> 00:08:44,634
results.

169
00:08:45,194 --> 00:08:49,284
Revenue was one hundred fifteen point eight
billion dollars for the quarter.

170
00:08:49,444 --> 00:08:51,284
More than double a year earlier.

171
00:08:51,284 --> 00:08:54,324
Profit was more than
sixty-five billion dollars.

172
00:08:54,324 --> 00:08:56,637
Both beat Wall Street's expectations.

173
00:08:56,637 --> 00:09:00,637
The company said it expected about one
hundred thirty billion dollars in

174
00:09:00,637 --> 00:09:02,317
revenue in the next quarter.

175
00:09:02,317 --> 00:09:05,967
And Kress said the plan Huang had
put in place was on track.

176
00:09:06,447 --> 00:09:11,007
After the report, the shares rose about
five percent in after-hours trading.

177
00:09:11,007 --> 00:09:13,247
One analyst summed it up this way.

178
00:09:13,247 --> 00:09:17,415
An orderly change at the top doesn't
have to destroy a company's value.

179
00:09:17,735 --> 00:09:20,295
But the questions didn't go away.

180
00:09:20,295 --> 00:09:21,655
They just changed.

181
00:09:21,655 --> 00:09:25,975
Nvidia's board created a new committee
to oversee national security and

182
00:09:25,975 --> 00:09:27,227
export rules.

183
00:09:27,227 --> 00:09:31,866
The US Treasury Secretary said the company
should hire its next chief executive

184
00:09:31,866 --> 00:09:32,883
from outside.

185
00:09:33,043 --> 00:09:37,283
And Nvidia's business in China, and maybe
in the rest of Asia, now depends

186
00:09:37,283 --> 00:09:39,923
partly on how Washington reacts.

187
00:09:40,483 --> 00:09:42,156
There's a strange irony here.

188
00:09:42,316 --> 00:09:45,916
Huang spent years arguing in Washington
that Nvidia should be allowed to

189
00:09:45,916 --> 00:09:48,956
sell more chips around the
world, including to China.

190
00:09:48,956 --> 00:09:53,516
Now the scandal around him could make
those rules tighter, not looser.

191
00:09:54,699 --> 00:09:57,659
Part 6 So, What Is One CEO Worth?

192
00:09:57,659 --> 00:09:59,659
So let's put it together.

193
00:09:59,979 --> 00:10:03,588
The chips, the customers and the
factories didn't change in November.

194
00:10:03,748 --> 00:10:06,628
The quarter was the best
Nvidia has ever had.

195
00:10:06,628 --> 00:10:10,708
What changed was the story investors
tell themselves about the future.

196
00:10:10,708 --> 00:10:15,347
And for a company priced on the future,
the story is most of the value.

197
00:10:15,747 --> 00:10:19,187
The seven hundred fifty billion
dollars didn't go to anyone.

198
00:10:19,187 --> 00:10:21,427
It was the price of uncertainty.

199
00:10:21,427 --> 00:10:22,547
Who leads now?

200
00:10:22,547 --> 00:10:24,547
Who makes the next big bet?

201
00:10:24,547 --> 00:10:26,025
What will Washington do?

202
00:10:26,185 --> 00:10:29,945
Some of that value could come back if
the answers turn out to be good.

203
00:10:29,945 --> 00:10:33,625
Some of it may have been the air
coming out of a much bigger boom.

204
00:10:34,105 --> 00:10:36,513
And one person is still at
the center of it.

205
00:10:36,513 --> 00:10:39,073
Jensen Huang has not been
charged with anything.

206
00:10:39,073 --> 00:10:42,833
The investigations are still open, and he
has said he wants to come back.

207
00:10:42,833 --> 00:10:46,193
Until the facts are clear, it's worth
remembering how much of this story

208
00:10:46,193 --> 00:10:49,090
is still reported, not proven.

209
00:10:49,489 --> 00:10:52,129
A company's value is a guess
about the future.

210
00:10:52,129 --> 00:10:55,649
When the person who has been writing that
future walks out, the guess gets

211
00:10:55,649 --> 00:10:56,849
cheaper.

212
00:10:57,802 --> 00:11:00,522
Next time, the company behind Google lost
about five hundred billion dollars

213
00:11:00,522 --> 00:11:02,866
in value in a single day, right
after its sales jumped.

214
00:11:02,866 --> 00:11:04,177
So what were investors afraid of?

215
00:11:04,337 --> 00:11:08,177
And if you want the other side of the
economy, the ships, planes and rockets

216
